Z||000000||btoth||05-24-2001||02:33 PM||||I was told that my right rear inner and outer tie rods need to be fixed... that they're "fused" together.  The guy that told me this basically said that the bushing is worn out.  Since the one side is messed up, I figure I might as well do both sides while I'm at it.<P>So, is it possible to just get the bushings?  Or do the inner and outer pieces actually need to be replaced?  I know nothing about this stuff obviously.<P>Here's the other thing:  NAPA has 2 outer tie rods in stock for $61.  But the Fiero Store sells their outer ones for 35.81.  What gives?  I would think that the Fiero Store would be the higher of the two.  The Fiero Store also sells the inner ones for $85.25 a piece... is this a rip off or sound right?<P>Any info would be appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrgone||05-24-2001||04:59 PM||||rear inners are very expensive<P>i bought mine from the dealer for 160$ canadian a peice||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||05-24-2001||06:23 PM||||It would make sense to replace both sides at the same time because you will be paying for a four wheel alignment anyways. As far as the prices I am not familiar-usually you get what you pay for. If you want cheap parts ask for "white box" parts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-24-2001||11:58 PM||||Be prepared. I think I just bought the last two in the US from GM. ~$100 ea (one piece inner and outer together. I wanted 4, but there were only two in the Reno warehouse and none anywhere else. A nationwide search might find some at a dealer. <P>I tried to find some McQual, Moog, or TRW and none were listed. If someone finds a number, let me know. I need two more.<P>I think this is a good opprotunity for Mr. Dickman to make an extension for some A car tierods. The Fiero's are very long in the back. Longer than anything I've seen in a rack. They are basically just rack and pinion inner tierods with a pin tack-welded on to keep them from spinning when you try and tighten the nut down.||||reg||1
